  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 PM MST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nTemperatures are on a warmer trend this week with lowland highs \nreaching at least 70 by the end of the work week. Higher \nelevations are cooler in the upper 50s, but this is still above \nnormal for this time of year. Easterly morning winds do become \nbreezy ahead of an upper level trough that shift the wind \ndirection out of the southwest by evening. That system moves in \nthrough the day tomorrow followed by some moisture that brings a \nslight chance of evening rain to the Gila and Black Range.\n\nBy Wednesday, winds become light and variable as high pressure \nbuilds into the area under an upper level ridge that keeps \nconditions fair and mild persisting through the middle of the \nweek. The next trough developing over the rockies creates \npredominant southwesterly flow on Friday. While the rain chances \nassociated with that system favor areas north of us, winds could \ngust between 30-40 mph bringing a cooler start to our weekend. The\ntrend we've established maintains itself as another ridge favors \ncalm and fair conditions over the weekend before our next \nnoteworthy rain chance enters the area early next week. It's far \nout to speak with certainty, but this system could bring up to 0.1\ninches of rain to areas of high elevation.",
